Reliable\nImplant Treatment (AMRIT) pharmacy Scheme across the country;\n(b) if so, the details , status and salient features thereof along with the name of the\ndiseases covered and the medicines proposed to be provided thereunder; and\n(c) the number of such pharmacies opened/proposed to be opened across the country,\nState/UT-wise including Maharashtra along with the time by which the said pharmacies\nare likely to be fully operational across the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 192* FOR 11TH DECEMBER, 2015\n(a) to (c): Affordable Medicines and Reliable Implant Treatment (AMRIT) pharmacy\nhas been opened at All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi and is\napproved for six new AIIMS and major Central Government hospitals. The State/UT\nwise list of the hospitals/institutions where such pharmacies are approved is placed at\nAnnexure 1.\nAMRIT pharmacies are to sell Cancer and Cardiovascular disease drugs and\nimplants to the patient s with valid prescriptions. The list of the medicine s and implants\nidentified for Cancer and Cardiovascular Diseases for AMRIT Outlet at AIIMS, New\nDelhi is placed at Annexure 2.\nState Governments have been informed regarding A MRIT pharmacies and M/s\nHLL Lifecare Ltd. has been directed to consider the request s of State Governments for\nopening of AMRIT pharmacies at major State Government hospitals/Institutions.\n\nAnnexure -I\nList of Hospitals where AMRIT Pharmacies are approved to be opened.\nS.\nNo. HOSPITAL STATE/UT\n1 Ram Manohar Lohia (RML) Hospital, New Delhi Delhi\n2. Lady Harding  Medical College (LHMC), New Delhi Delhi\n3. Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education &\nResearch (PGIMER)\nChandigarh\n4. Jawaharlal Institute of Postgraduate Medical\nEducation & Research(JIPMER)\nPuducherry\n5. Regional Institute of Medical Sciences (NEIGRIHMS),\nShillong\nMeghalaya\n6 Regional Institute of Medical Sciences,  Imphal Manipur\n7. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Bhopal Madhya\nPradesh\n8. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS),\nBhubaneshwar\nOdisha\n9. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS),\nJodhpur\nRajasthan\n10. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Patna Bihar\n11. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Raipur Chhattisgarh\n12. All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS),\nRishikesh\nUttrakhand\n\nANNEXURE 2\nCARDIO-VASCULAR DRUGS\nS.No Item Name Tab/Syp/Caps/Inj. Strength\n1 Acenocoumarol Tab 1mg\n2 Acenocoumarol Tab 3mg\n3 Acenocoumarol Tab 4mg\n4 Acenocoumarol Tab 2 mg\n5 Acetyl Salicylic Acid Tab. 350 mg\n6 ACETYLCYSTEINE 20% Inj. 400mg 2ml\n7 Actyl salicylic acid + cacium\nCarbonate+ Anhydrous ctitric acid Tab 325mg+100mg+35mg\n8 Adenocine Inj. 6mg 2ml\n9 Alteplase Tissue Plasminogen Inj. 50 mg\n10 Alteplase Tissue Plasminogen Inj. 20 mg\n11 Aminocaproic Acid Inj. 250 mg/ml (20ml)\n12 Amiodarone Tab. 100mg\n13 Amiodarone Hcl Inj. 150mg 3ml\n14 Amlodepine Tab. 5mg\n15 Amlodepine Tab. 2.5mg\n16 Amlodipine5mg+Atenolol 50mg Tab. 5mg+50mg\n17 Aprotinin Inj. 70mg 50ml\n18 Aprotinin BP Inj. 10000 KIU 5 ml vial\n19 Aspirin Tab. 100mg\n20 Aspirin ASA Tab. 150mg\n21 Aspirin ASA Tab. 75mg\n22 Atenolol Tab. 50mg\n23 Atenolol Tab. 25mg\n24 Atorvastatin Tab. 10mg\n25 Atorvastatin Tab. 20 mg\n26 Atorvastatin Tab. 40mg\n27 Atorvastatin Tab. 80mg\n28 Bisoprolol Tab. 2.5mg\n29 Bisoprolol Tab. 5mg\n30 Bosentan Tab. 62.5 mg\n31 Bosentan Tab. 125mg\n32 Carvedilol Tab. 3.125 mg\n33 Carvedilol Tab. 6.25 mg\n34 Carvedilol Tab. 12.5mg\n35 Chlorthalidone Tab. 12.5mg\n36 Cilostazol Tab 100mg\n37 Cinnarizine Tab. 25mg\n38 Cilnidipine Tab. 5mg\n39 Cilnidipine Tab. 10mg\n40 Clonidine Tab. 0.1 mg\n41 Clonidine Tab. 0.2 mg\n42 Clonidine Inj. 150 mcg 1ml\n43 Clopidogrel Tab. 300mg\n44 Clopidrogrel Tab. 75mg\n\n45 Clopidrogrel + asprin ASA Tab. 75mg+75mg\n46\nCardioplegia Solution 1000 ML M\nmol/L NaCI 15, KCL 9 , Mg SO4 6\nH2O 4 , Hislidine HCI H2O 18 ,\nHISTIDINE 180, TRYPTOPHAN 2 ,\nMANNITOL 30 , Ca CI2 2 H2O 0.15 ,\nK, H, 2 KETOGLUTA 1 , RATE\nOSMOLARITY 310 MOSMOL/ 1000\nml Solution\nSolution 1000 ml\n47 Dalteparin Inj. 5000 I.U. 0.2ml\n48 Digoxin Inj. 0.5mg 2ml\n49 Digoxin Syp. 3mg/5ml (60ml)\n50 Digoxin Tab. 0.25mg\n51 Diltiazem Inj. 25mg 5ml\n52 Diltiazem Tab. 30mg\n53 Diltiazem Tab. 90mg SR\n54 Dipyridamole Tab 100mg\n55 Dobutamine hcl Inj. 250 mg 20ml\n56 Dopamine Inj. 40mg 5ml\n57 Enalapril Tab. 2.5mg\n58 Enalapril Tab. 5mg\n59 Enoxaparin Inj. 80mg/0.8ml\n60 Enoxaparin l, PF Syringe Inj. 40mg 0.4ml\n61 Enoxaparin l, PF Syringe Inj. 60mg 0.6ml\n62 Eplerenone Tab. 25"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |193", "t": "Cooperative Banks", "head": "Government of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial Services\nLOK SABHA\nStarred Question No. †*193\nTo be answered on December 11, 2015/Agrahayana 20, 1937(Saka)\nCooperative Banks\nQuestion\n†*193. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of Cooperative Banks registered in the country as on date, State/ UT-wise;\n(b) whether the total assets of the Cooperative Banks and the share thereof in the banking\nsector is not more than five per cent and if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether most of the Cooperative Banks are facing difficulties and have incurred losses\ndue to short term loan distributed to the farmers and also inclusion of their agricultural\nincome into tax net and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether the Government proposes to compensate and provide tax rebate to these\nbanks and if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Rajasthan; and\n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to revive the loss making Cooperative\nBanks?\nAnswer\nThe Minister of Finance\n(Shri Arun Jaitley)\n(a) to (e):- A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n**********\n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *193 to\nbe answered on 11 th December, 2015 regarding Cooperative Banks by Shri Sukhbir Singh\nJaunapuria.\nThere are 1576 Urban Cooperative Banks(UCBs) in the country as on date. The number of Rural\nCooperative Banks is 403, which include 33 State Cooperative Banks(StCBs) and 370 District\nCentral Cooperative Banks(DCCBs). The State-wise list of Urban Cooperative Banks(UCBs), as\nreported by Reserve Bank of India, is given in Annexure-I. As regards the Rural Cooperative\nBanks, the State-wise lists of State Cooperative Banks (StCBs) and District Central Co-operative\nBanks (DCCBs), as reported by National Bank for Agriculture & Rural Development (NABARD),\nare given in Annexure-II & III respectively.\nNABARD has reported that as on 31.3.2015, the total assets of the Indian Banking Sector\n(excluding Urban Cooperative Banks) constituted Rs.13054831.00 crore, out of which the total\nassets of Rural Cooperative Banks, i.e., StCBs and DCCBs account for Rs.607809.00 crore,\nconstituting 4.66%. RBI has reported that the total assets of the UCB sector, as on 31.3.2015,\nwere Rs.4,35,197 crore.\nUnder the Interest Subvention Scheme for Short Term Crop Loans upto Rs.3.00 lakh, the\nCooperative Banks are given a subvention of 2% by the Government in respect of their own\ninvolved funds, for providing crop loans at 7% to farmers. Further, the cooperative banks get short\nterm refinance from NABARD at the interest rate of 4.5% to the extent of 30%-60% (based on\nfactors such as NPA level, location, etc.) of ground level credit flow. Thus, NABARD’s refinance\nallows cooperative banks to earn 0.5% more as compared to the cases when their own funds are\ninvolved.\nSection 80P of the Income-tax Act, 1961 presently provides hundred per cent deduction to co-\noperative societies of profits and gains of business attributable to marketing of agricultural produce\ngrown by its members, purchase of agricultural implements, seeds, livestock or other articles\nintended for agriculture for the purpose of supplying them to its members, processing, without the\naid of power, of the agricultural produce of its members. Besides 100% deduction of profits and\ngains of a primary cooperative society from supply of milk, oilseed, fruits or vegetables raised or\ngrown by its members to federal cooperative society engaged in same business is also available\nunder section 80P of the Income tax Act. At present, there is no proposal to provide any further\nexemption.\n\nIn order to infuse capital in the Cooperative Banks, State Governments can avail of long term loans\nfrom NABARD up to a cap of 25% towards Share Capital contribution to Cooperative Banks.\nSome of the other important measures by RBI/NABARD for improving the financial viability of\nCooperative Banks are as under:\n(i) The Cooperatives have been advised to raise capital by increasing their membership, to\nissue Long term (Subordinated) Debts (LTDs) and Innovative Perpetual Debt Instruments\n(IPDIs) permitted by RBI.\n(ii) Fit & Proper criteria has been prescribed by RBI for appointment of Chief Executive\nOfficers (CEOs) in Cooperative Banks. All States have been advised to amend their Acts\nas per the Constitutional (97 th) Amendment Act, 2011 to appoint minimum two professional\ndirectors on the boards of cooperative banks.\n(iii) Continuous efforts by RBI/ NABARD and other stakeholders have resulted in Cooperative\nBanks implementing Core Banking Solution (CBS).\n(iv) Training of the staff in the new products being offered through CBS by making use of the\ntraining facilities of the Cooperative Training Institutes (CTIs), which are financially\nsupported by NABARD.\n****\n\nAnnexure I referred to in reply to SQ 193 for 11.12.2015\nAnnex - I\nState and Union Territory-wise distribution of UCBs Position as on dated (04."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |194", "t": "Disinvestment PSUs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DISINVESTMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *194 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11th DECEMBER, 2015 \nAGRAHAYANA 20, 1937 (SAKA) \n  \nDisinvestment in PSUs \n \n*194 ADV. NARENDRA KESHAV SAWAIKAR: \n SHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  the salient features of the present disinvestment policy of the Government; \n(b) Whether the Government has taken note of reservations expressed over \ndisinvestment policy, if so, the response of the Government thereon; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to devise new guidelines to disinvest loss \nmaking Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSEs)/Public Sector Undertakings \n(PSUs); \n(d) If so, the details of CPSEs/PSUs/banks disinvested/likely to be \ndisinvested/funds collected during each of the last three years and the current \nyear, CPSE/PSU/Equity-wise; and \n(e) whether the Government proposes to utili se the above funds in new \nGovernment enterprises and also plan to infuse it as capital in loss making \nPSUs, if so, the details thereof and the steps taken thereon? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  FINANCE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ARUN JAITLEY) \n \n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 194 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11TH \nDECEMBER, 2015 REGARDING ‘ DISINVESTMENT IN PSUs ’ \n \n(a) The current disinvestment policy approved by the Government on 5 th \nNovember, 2009 envisages development of people’s ownership of Central Public \nSector Enterprises (CPSEs) so as to share in their wealth and prosperity, while \nensuring that the Government equity does not fall below 51% and Government retains \nmanagement cont rol. The salient features of the present disinvestment policy of the \nGovernment are as follows: \n \n(i) Listed  CPSEs ( not meeting SEBI’s minimum mandatory shareholding of \n10% which now stand revised to 25% ) are to be made compliant through sale \nof shares by Government or by the CPSEs through issue of fresh shares or a \ncombination of both;  \n(ii) Unlisted CPSEs with  no accumulated losses and having earned net profit in \nthree consecutive preceding years are to be listed. \n(iii) In case of profit making  minority stake sale  (disinvestment upto 49% equity \nof Central Public Sector Enterprises), management control of Central Public \nSector Enterprises (CPSEs) with 51 % will remain with the government;  \n \n(b) The Trade Unions in the past have  expressed concern over  disinvestment in \nCPSEs . In this regard, it has been clarified that disinvestment is being done as per the \nextant Government policy and SEBI guidelines.  \n \n(c) In the budget speech of 2015 -16, it has been stated that di sinvestment would \ninclude both disinvestment in loss making units, and some strategic disinvestment. \nPursuant to this announcement, inter-ministerial consultations are ongoing. \n \n (d)   The details of the CPSEs/ PSUs disinvestment during the last 3 years and the \ncurrent year is as follows: \nFinancial Year 2012-13  \n \nS.No. Name of CPSE Receipts (in Rs. \ncrore) \n1. National Building Construction Corporation  124.97 \n2. Hindustan Copper Ltd. 807.03 \n3. NMDC Ltd. 5,973.27 \n4. Oil India Ltd. 3,141.51 \n5. NTPC Ltd. 11,457.54 \n6. Rashtriya Chemicals and Fertilizers Ltd. (RCF) 310.15 \n7. National Aluminium Company Ltd. (NALCO) 627.84 \n8. Steel Authority of India Ltd. (SAIL) 1,514.50 \n\nTotal 23,956.81 \n \n \n \nFinancial Year 2013-14 \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of CPSEs Receipts (in Rs. \nCrore) \n1 Hindustan Copper Ltd.(HCL) 259.56 \n2 ITDC Ltd. 30.17 \n3 MMTC Ltd. 571.71 \n4 National Fertilizers Ltd. (NFL) 101.08 \n5 State Trading Corporation Ltd.(STC) 4.54 \n6 Neyveli Lignite Corporation Ltd. (NLC) 358.21 \n7 Engineers India Ltd. (EIL) 497.32 \n8 Indian Oil Corporation Ltd. (IOCL) 5,341.49 \n9 CPSE-Exchange Traded Fund 3,000 \n10 National Hydroelectric Power Corporation (NHPC) 2,131.28 \n11 Power Grid Corporation of India Ltd. (PGCIL) 1,637.32 \n12 Bharat Heavy Electricals Ltd. (BHEL) 1,886.78 \n Total 15,819.46 \n \nFinancial Year 2014-15 \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of CPSEs Receipts (in Rs. Crore) \n1 SAIL 1,719.54 \n2 Coal India Ltd.(CIL) 22,557.63 \n Total 24,277.17* \n* An additional amount of Rs.71.54 crore has also been realized through employees \nOFS. \n \nFinancial Year 2015-16   \n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of CPSEs Receipts (in Rs. Crore) \n1 Rural Electrification Corporation Ltd. (REC) 1,608.00 \n2 Power Finance Corporation Ltd. (PFC) 1,671.00 \n3 Dredging Corporation of India Ltd.(DCIL) 53.33 \n4 Indian Oil Corporation Ltd. (IOC) 9,369.00 \n Total 12701.33 \n \nThe Government has also identified some CPSEs for disinvestment during the year in \nsectors, which includes defence, fertilizer, heavy engineering, minerals and metals, \noil, power, steel, technical consultancy, tourism, transport services, trading and \n\nmarketing. The actual realiza tion from disinvestment of a CPSE depends on the \nprevailing market conditions and extant policy con"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |195", "t": "Promotion of AYUSH", "head": "       GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n               MINISTRY OF MINES \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.195 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2015 \nBAUXITE MINING LEASES TO GMDC \n \n195.  SHRI VINOD LAKHAMASHI CHAVDA: \n \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) whether the Government of Gujarat has recommended 11 bauxite mining \nlease proposals for prior approval of Ministry of Mines in Jamnagar and Kutch \ndistricts for relaxation of section 6(1) (b) of MMRD to Ministry of Mines;  \n(b) if so, the details of the action taken by Ministry of Mines in this regard; \n(c) whether MD, GMDC and Principal Secretary, Industry and Mines Department, \nGovernment of Gujarat have requested Government of India to expedite the \napproval of the above bauxite mining leases in favour of GMDC; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the time by which the approval is likely to be \naccorded? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR MINES AND STEEL (SHRI VISHNU DEO SAI) \n  \n(a) :  Yes, 11 bauxite mining lease proposals for mines in Jamnagar and Kutch \ndistricts for prior approval and relaxation of section 6(1) (b) of the Mines and \nMineral Development and Regulation (MMRD) Act, 1957 were received in Ministry \nof Mines. \n(b) Out of 11 proposals, 4 proposals have been returned to the State Government \nas mineralization in the subject area was not established . Remaining 7 proposals \nhave been examined in this Ministry and additional information has been sought  \nfrom State Government.  \n(c) Yes, Principal Secretary, Industry and Mines Department, Government of \nGujarat has requested Central Government to expedite its approval for the above \nbauxite mining leases in favour of GMDC;  \n(d) The time for taking decision in the matter cannot be specified.  \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |196", "t": "Public Health Standards", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 196\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH DECEMBER 2015\nPUBLIC HEALTH STANDARDS\n*196. SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has laid down Public Health Standards in the country,\nif so, the details and the objectives thereof;\n(b) whether the guidelines issued by the Government under the said standards are\nimplemented in the country, if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons\ntherefor; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government to ensure better implementation of the said\nstandards in the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 196* FOR 11TH DECEMBER, 2015\nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare has published the Indian Public\nHealth Standards (IPHS) for the various levels of health facilities [District\nHospital, Sub -district hospital, Community Health Centre, Primary Health\nCentre and Sub-centre.] in January/February 2007 and then revised in 2012.\nThe Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) are a set of uniform standards\nenvisaged to improve the quality of health care delivery in the country and the\nStates are encouraged to use them as reference points for public health care\ninfrastructure planning and up-gradation of health facilities.\nUnder the National Health Mission (NHM), which subsumes the NRHM\nand the NUHM as its Sub -Missions, support is provided for States/UTs for\nupgradation of facilities to Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS) based on\nrequirements projected by the States/UTs in their Programme Implementation\nPlans (PIPs)."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |197", "t": "Bio-Medical Research", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH REASEARCH\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 197\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 11TH DECEMBER 2015\nBIO-MEDICAL RESEARCH\n*197. SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:\nKUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has recently approved the proposal for setting up of a\nNational Resource Facility for BioMedical Research (NARF) in the country;\n(b) if so, the details and t he objectives thereof along with the estimated cost to be\nincurred on setting up of the said research institute;\n(c) the details of the activities envisaged to be undertaken by NARF in the field of\nbio-medical research and the manner in which it is likely to function;\n(d) whether the Government proposes to set up similar research institute in other\nparts of the country, and if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) the other measures taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to promote\nbio-medical research in the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 197* FOR 11TH DECEMBER, 2015\n(a): Government has approved on 18.11.2015 a proposal of the Department of\nHealth Research for ‘establishment of a National Animal Resource Facility\n(NARF) at Hyderabad for biomedical research.\n(b): The facility would be raised in an area of 102.69 acre s of land at the cost of\nRs. 338.58 crores. The objectives are to create facilities for breeding and housing\nof animals such as primates, canines and other specialized models such as\ntransgenic and knockout rodents, to provide technological resources for biomedical\nresearch.\n(c): The activities envisaged to be undertaken in the facility include breeding and\nhousing of a variety of animals to facilitate biomedical research. This facility\nwould also function as an internationally accredited testing laborato ry for\ndevelopment of drugs and quality control of various products through research.\n(d) There is no other proposal to set up a similar NARF in other parts of the\ncountry.\n(e): ICMR is conducting biomedical research in the country through in house an d\nexternal research projects. The in -house research is being conducted through the\nnetwork of ICMR’s permanent institutes and Regional Centres. Activities like\naward of research fellowships and international collaboration are also undertaken\nto improve the research capabilities of the country.\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |198", "t": "Malnutrition among Women and Children", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 198 \n TO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2015 \n \nWELFARE FUNDS FOR STs IN NE STATES \n \n198.      SHRI SIRAJUDDIN AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of funds for welfare of Scheduled Tribes (STs) provided by the Central \nBudget and other heads to North-East (NE) States including Assam during each of the \nlast three years, State-wise; \n \n(b) whether the Union Government monitors utilization of the  central assistance and see \nthat the funds allocated are spent in the same head under which it has been allocated; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether any report of spending the central assistance i.e. fund meant for one head \nbeing spent for another head has been received by the Government; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the reaction of the Government thereto; and \n \n(f) the corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI MANSUKHBHAI DHANJIBHAI VASAVA) \n \n(a): Budget allocation made to the Ministry of Tribal Affairs for the various welfare \nschemes of scheduled tribes during the last three years is given as under:  \n                           (Rs. in crore) \nYear BE (including NE States) \n2012-13 4090.00 \n2013-14 4279.00 \n2014-15 4479.00 \n No State-wise allocation of funds is made for most of the Schemes administered by \nthis Ministry. Funds are released to States as per the guidelines of the respective schemes of \nthe Ministry. Funds release d to North East States including Assam under some of the \nimportant schemes of the Ministry is annexed. \n\n \n \nVarious Central Ministries/ Departments are mandated to earmark certain percentage \nof funds out of their total outlay under the Tribal Sub Plan (TSP). Th e total TSP allocation by \nvarious Central Ministries/ Departments during the last three years is given as under: \n                           (Rs. in crore) \nYear BE  \n2012-13 21710.11 \n2013-14 24598.39 \n2014-15 32386.84 \n \nAll Central Ministries/ Departments are also required to set aside 10% of the annual \nfunds for the development of the North Eastern States including Assam. \n(b) to (f):  It is mandatory for various Central Ministries/ Departments to earmark \ncertain percentage of funds out of their total outla y under the Tribal Sub Plan (TSP) which is \nnon-divertible. It is also mandatory for all Central Ministries/ Departments to set aside 10% \nof the annual funds for the development of the North Eastern States including Assam which \nis non-divertible and non-lapsable as well. No divergence of funds has come to the notice of \nthis Ministry.  \nFunds under the schemes administered by the Ministry are released only on receipt of \ncomplete proposal, utilization certificate, progress report, etc. Implementation of the \nprogrammes and utilization of funds is also monitored through official visits, \nmeeting/conference with State government officials. The Ministry also issued Operational \nGuidelines for Formulation, Implementation and Monitoring of TSP funds and Grants under \nArticle 275(1) for effective implementation of schemes administered by the Ministry.  \nThe Ministry of Tribal Affairs, being the nodal Ministry for the development of scheduled \ntribes, is regularly monitoring the utilization of funds for the welfare of scheduled tribes \nthrough an Inter -Ministerial Coordination Committee with a view  to en sure better \nconvergence of financial resources and optimize the results under TSP components of various \nCentral Ministries/Departments.  \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure \nFunds released to North East States including Assam under some of the important Schemes of the Ministry of Tribal Affairs \n1) Grants-in-aid under Article 275 (1) of the Constitution  \n       (Rs. In lakhs) \nSl.No. Name of State \nFunds Released \n2012-13 \n \n2013-14 2014-15 \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 0.00 832.19 1880.40 \n2 Assam 0.00 3540.25 0.00 \n3 Manipur 1031.00 1031.00 1600.00 \n4 Meghalaya 0.00 2924.38 2334.03 \n5 Mizoram 810.75 1133.61 1877.78 \n6 Nagaland 2454.00 2886.93 2067.15 \n7 Sikkim 272.58 302.90 370.30 \n8 Tripura 1375.00 1355.00 1218.99 \n \n2) Special Central Assistance to Tribal Sub-Plan (SCA to TSP) \n                                                                                                                 (Rs. In lakhs) \nS.No. Name of State \nFunds Released \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 \n1 Assam 4674.00 6563.63 1788.58 \n2 Manipur 1230.00 1581.90 1118.00 \n3 Sikkim 437.00 437.00 520.25 \n4 Tripura \n \n1955.00 \n \n2102.10 \n \n1183.94 \n     \n \n3) Institutional Support for Development and Marketing of Tribal Products/Produce \n                                                                                                              (Rs. in lakhs) \nS. \nNo. \nName of the State Funds Released \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 \n1. Assam  -- -- -- \n2. Meghalaya -- 106.00 -- \n3. Tripura 52.00 54.00 119.93 \n4. Mizoram  24.00 45.00 -- "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|6|STARRED   |199", "t": "Cases of Diabetes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 199 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30th NOVEMBER, 2015 \n \n“SETTING UP OF NEW MUSEUMS UNDER MUSEUM GRANT SCHEME” \n199.  SHRI OM BIRLA: \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of number of new museums built by State Governments and other \nbodies in the country under the Museum Grant Scheme in the last three years, \nState-wise; and  \n \n(b) the details of institutions that received Museum Grant Scheme for maintaining \nexisting museums ? \n \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) CULTURE & TOURIS  \n \nAND  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR CIVIL AVIATION \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA) \n \n \n(a) The details are at Annexure - A  \n \n \n(b) The details are at Annexure – B   \n\n \nANNEXURE-A \n \nDETAILS OF NUMBER OF NEW MUSEUMS BUILT BY STATE GOVERNMENTS AND OTHER BODIES IN THE \nCOUNTRY UNDER THE MUSEUM GRANT SCHEME IN THE LAST THREE YEARS   \nS.No. State Details of Proposals Funds Released (In lakhs) \n  2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 \n1.  J